//! THE REFERENCE COUNT, AND WHAT ZERO DOES (sawos design 7 D-1/D-2) — the
//! per-kind matrix in one place, on `waitables`' own model.
//!
//! Every countable slab slot carries a `refs`, and everything that can be said
//! about maintaining it is one of three questions, each answered by an
//! EXHAUSTIVE match on `ObjType`:
//!
//!   `ref_object`     something now names this object
//!   `unref_object`   something no longer does — and zero FREES, right here
//!   `free_object`    the slot goes back, and the kind's last rites run
//!
//! That is a MATRIX rather than a funnel because the answers genuinely differ
//! per kind — an Interrupt's last rites mask a device line, a Timer's
//! reprogram the comparator, a Waiter's detach a list AND WAKE WHAT IS PARKED
//! ON IT (sawos design 12), a Mapping's deliberately leave a protection row
//! installed — and it is written as three functions in one place so the matrix
//! is READABLE AS ONE. A kind added to `ObjType` fails
//! all three to compile until somebody says what references it, which is the
//! property a `_` arm anywhere here would destroy.
//!
//! **AND M4 UNIT 1 IS WHERE THAT PROPERTY EARNED ITS KEEP** (sawos design 13).
//! `PipeInlet` and `PipeOutlet` are two counted kinds over ONE connection slot
//! with TWO reference columns, and it was `drop_reference`'s demand for an
//! answer to "was that the last" — for an object with two names — that turned
//! D-1's both-zero-frees from a sentence in a brief into a property of the
//! ledger. Each arm decrements ITS column and then answers about the PAIR, so
//! `unref_object`'s "zero frees" means "both-zero frees" here without that
//! function knowing two kinds are involved. The two are also the only arms in
//! this file with something to say when a count reaches zero WITHOUT freeing:
//! one column at zero is the PEER-GONE transition, and `free_object` runs only
//! when both are, so there is no other site it could be written at.
//!
//! **AND M4 UNIT 2 REPEATED THE WHOLE SHAPE ONE LEVEL DOWN** (sawos design 14).
//! `PipeReply` and `PipeRequest` — the client's claim on a reply and the server's
//! obligation to send one — are two counted kinds over one RING SLOT with two
//! reference columns, exactly as the endpoint pair is over one connection: each
//! arm decrements its column and answers about the whole exchange, and reaching
//! zero on one column alone is the ABANDONMENT transition that `Reply` and
//! `Resolve` read off the counts. The one thing that is not the endpoint pair's
//! rule verbatim is `exchange_can_settle`'s extra clause — a STAGED message is
//! still owed to a live outlet whatever became of its claim, which is §2.1's TELL
//! idiom — and it is written there rather than here.
//!
//! **THE TEARDOWN'S FORCE-FREE IS GONE** (`designs/010` ruling 10, design 14
//! D-3): `end_process` no longer sweeps the Memory, IoMemory and Pipe slabs by
//! charged process. `reap_unreferenced` below is what took its place and the
//! block above it carries the argument.
//!
//! # AND M4 UNIT 3 MOVED THE DELIVERY IN HERE (sawos design 15)
//!
//! `kcore.wake` used to be its own module, one line above this one: readiness
//! becoming a woken thread. It is now the second half of this file, and the
//! reason is that the two became MUTUALLY RECURSIVE and a module boundary
//! cannot hold a cycle (DF-232e — an import cycle is not diagnosed, it silently
//! empties a module's export table and blames a third file).
//!
//! Both directions arrived with `designs/010`'s M4 unit-3 rulings, and each is
//! a sentence of one of them:
//!
//!   - **A DELIVERY NOW DROPS A REFERENCE.** Ruling 9(b)'s `AttachMode.OneShot`
//!     detaches AT DELIVERY, and ruling 11(b) makes a REPLY delivery detach
//!     whatever mode was passed — and an attachment is a counted reference
//!     (design 7 D-1), so `deliver_attachment` unrefs. That is delivery
//!     reaching into the ledger.
//!   - **A DROPPED REFERENCE NOW DELIVERS.** The peer-gone terminal levels
//!     (`designs/010` ruling 2's doctrine: nothing parked can be silently
//!     doomed) fire exactly when a column reaches zero, and the only function
//!     that observes a column reaching zero is `drop_reference` below. That is
//!     the ledger reaching into delivery.
//!
//! What did NOT change is the rule `kcore`'s facade states about where a seam
//! lives: the STATE of a thing sits below the process teardown and ACTING on it
//! sits above. The wait TABLES are still `waitables`, one module down, and this
//! file is still the acting half — it simply now does both kinds of acting,
//! because they turned out to be one act. Its altitude is unchanged: it needs
//! `process`'s copy door, which it sits above, and nothing above itself.
//!
//! # ZERO FREES, SYNCHRONOUSLY (D-1)
//!
//! The kernel is one context running to completion, so the count hits zero
//! INSIDE the very syscall that dropped the last reference and the free is a
//! plain call: no deferred reclamation, no cleanup queue, nothing to drain at a
//! later point. Cascades compose out of the same two functions — releasing a
//! Waiter to zero detaches its attachments, which may drop an Event to zero,
//! which frees and credits — and they terminate because an attachment is
//! counted on the WAITABLE and never on the Waiter, so the graph is a forest.
//!
//! WHAT ZERO DOES NOT FREE IS A RANGE. A freed Memory slot returns to the slab
//! and its bytes return to no pool: the front-cut parent cannot absorb an
//! arbitrary hole (the one-`{base,len}` shape, design 6 D-2). Quotas count
//! OBJECTS, not bytes, in v1; byte accounting arrives with a real allocator,
//! and §2.5's pages-free-on-last-reference clause is narrowed accordingly in
//! the spec.
//!
//! # WHY THIS IS A MODULE AND WHERE IT SITS
//!
//! Between `process` and `wake`, and the call graph is what puts it there. It
//! has to reach EVERY slab — the two memory slabs and the handle table below in
//! `objects`, the Timer slab in `time`, the three wait slabs in `waitables` —
//! and also `PROCESSES`, because a Process's own count is what design 3 D-3's
//! reclaim now reads. Written any lower it could not see the process table;
//! written any higher it would be above the teardown that also frees these
//! slabs. It reaches nothing above itself.
//!
//! Two mints are NOT expressed through this matrix and could not be: the boot
//! path's, in `process`, which sits below this file. They cost nothing to write
//! out — a boot Memory and IoMemory are created with `refs: 1` in the slot
//! literal (a fresh object's first reference is not a change to a count, it IS
//! the count) and root's own Process handle is one increment on its own table.
//! Every GENERIC site — where a handle is bound to a kind the code does not
//! know — comes through here.

import hal
import sosabi.{EventMode, ObjType, PIPE_BODY_BYTES, ReplyLevel, SosStatus,
               WAIT_HEADER_WORDS, WaitTag, wait_header_bytes}
import kcore.limits.{MAX_IOMEMORIES, MAX_MEMORIES, MAX_PIPES, MAX_THREADS,
                     NO_THREAD, PIPE_INFLIGHT}
import kcore.diag.{ExitCode, fatal, fatal_kernel}
import kcore.result.{op_ok, op_status, op_value, write_result}
import kcore.threads.{THREADS, ThreadState, frame_slot, ready_push}
import kcore.objects.{
    ExchangeState,
    IOMEMORIES,
    IoMemorySlot,
    MAPPINGS,
    MEMORIES,
    MappingSlot,
    MemorySlot,
    NO_EXCHANGE,
    NO_ROW,
    PIPES,
    PIPE_CALLER,
    PIPE_EXCHANGES,
    PIPE_EX_STATE,
    PIPE_LENS,
    PIPE_REPLY_ATTACH,
    PIPE_REPLY_REFS,
    PIPE_REQUEST_ATTACH,
    PIPE_REQUEST_REFS,
    QuotaKind,
    SlotState,
    alloc_exchange,
    exchange_body_addr,
    exchange_can_settle,
    exchange_pipe,
    exchange_settle,
    free_pipe,
    pipe_drop_staged,
    pipe_exchange_at,
    pipe_idle,
    quota_credit,
    stage_push,
}
import kcore.time.{TIMERS, TimerSlot, reprogram_timer}
import kcore.waitables.{
    ATTACHMENTS,
    Attachment,
    EVENTS,
    EventSlot,
    INTERRUPTS,
    InterruptSlot,
    WAITERS,
    WaitAnswer,
    WaitableKind,
    WaiterSlot,
    accumulate,
    call_room_waiter,
    clear_pending_call,
    tie_call_reply,
    detach_attachment,
    set_waitable_attachment,
    waitable_answer,
    waitable_attachment,
    waitable_consume,
    waitable_ready,
}
import kcore.process.{PROCESSES, copy_in, copy_out, reclaim_process_slot}

/// Something now names object `slot` of kind `kind`.
///
/// Called BESIDE every generic bind: `mint_handle` at each op that hands out a
/// handle onto an object that already existed, `give`'s child-side bind, and
/// `waiter_add`'s attachment (through `ref_waitable`). A creation site does not
/// call it — a fresh slot is written with `refs: 1`, because its first
/// reference is the count rather than a change to one.
///
/// FOUR KINDS ARE NOT COUNTED, and each arm says which reason it is:
///
///   - `System` has no slab at all. It is the singleton every process's boot
///     handle names, so there is no slot to return and nothing to return it to.
///   - `Clock` is EXEMPT — kernel-eternal, owned by nobody (the Aug-17 user
///     ruling). Freeing a domain's slot on a release would take the machine's
///     monotonic counter away from everybody else.
///   - `Thread` is NOT COUNTED IN v1: the join/exit protocol owns a thread
///     slot's lifetime and does it on terms a handle count cannot express
///     (see `ThreadSlot`). Recorded, deferred.
///   - `Unbound` names nothing, so a bind of it is not a thing that happens.
public(package) func ref_object(kind: ObjType, slot: Int) unsafe {
    match kind {
        case Event -> { EVENTS[slot].refs = EVENTS[slot].refs + 1 },
        case Waiter -> { WAITERS[slot].refs = WAITERS[slot].refs + 1 },
        case Interrupt -> { INTERRUPTS[slot].refs = INTERRUPTS[slot].refs + 1 },
        case Timer -> { TIMERS[slot].refs = TIMERS[slot].refs + 1 },
        case Memory -> { MEMORIES[slot].refs = MEMORIES[slot].refs + 1 },
        case IoMemory -> { IOMEMORIES[slot].refs = IOMEMORIES[slot].refs + 1 },
        case Mapping -> { MAPPINGS[slot].refs = MAPPINGS[slot].refs + 1 },
        case Process -> { PROCESSES[slot].refs = PROCESSES[slot].refs + 1 },
        // **TWO KINDS, ONE SLOT, TWO COLUMNS** (sawos design 13 D-1). The slot
        // index is the CONNECTION's; the kind is what says which end this
        // handle names, and therefore which column it counts on. That is the
        // whole of D-1's mechanism, and it is three lines because the kind was
        // already established before this function was reached (§3's order).
        case PipeInlet -> { PIPES[slot].inlet_refs = PIPES[slot].inlet_refs + 1 },
        case PipeOutlet -> { PIPES[slot].outlet_refs = PIPES[slot].outlet_refs + 1 },
        // **THE SAME MECHANISM ONE LEVEL DOWN** (sawos design 14 D-1). `slot` is
        // an EXCHANGE — a connection's ring slot — and the kind is what says
        // whether this handle is the client's CLAIM or the server's OBLIGATION,
        // and therefore which of the exchange's two columns it counts on. Two
        // kinds over one slot, decided by `obj_type`, exactly as the two
        // endpoint kinds are over the connection above.
        case PipeReply -> { PIPE_REPLY_REFS[slot] = PIPE_REPLY_REFS[slot] + 1 },
        case PipeRequest -> {
            PIPE_REQUEST_REFS[slot] = PIPE_REQUEST_REFS[slot] + 1
        },
        case System -> {},
        case Clock -> {},
        case Thread -> {},
        case Unbound -> {},
    }
}

/// Whether connection `slot` may be given back NOW: no endpoint names it, and no
/// exchange lives in it.
///
/// **THE THIRD CLAUSE IS M4 UNIT 2's** (sawos design 14 D-1). A `PipeReply` or a
/// `PipeRequest` is a reference on a RING SLOT, and the ring slots live inside
/// the `PIPES` row — so freeing the row while a claim is live would hand that
/// claim a recycled connection. `pipe_idle` is what makes "the connection
/// outlives its endpoints for exactly as long as an exchange inside it does"
/// true, which is design 10 ruling 10's own sentence one level down.
///
/// **AND THE STATE TEST IS NOT DEFENSIVE.** Settling an exchange can CASCADE into
/// freeing the connection (`exchange_settle`), and the outlet's zero-arm settles
/// exchanges before it asks this question — so by the time it is asked the row
/// may already be back on the slab, and the honest answer for "should the caller
/// free it" is then no.
func pipe_frees_now(slot: Int) unsafe -> Bool {
    match PIPES[slot].state {
        case Free -> false,
        case Live -> {
            (PIPES[slot].inlet_refs == 0 && PIPES[slot].outlet_refs == 0
             && pipe_idle(slot))
        },
    }
}

/// Take one reference away, and answer whether that was the last.
///
/// SPLIT OUT FROM `unref_object` FOR ONE CALLER — the teardown, which must
/// decrement without freeing. See `unref_teardown` for why that is not an
/// inconsistency.
///
/// The zero test before the subtraction is not defensive bookkeeping: a
/// teardown zeroes the slots a process owned, so a LATER decrement from another
/// process's close-all can arrive at a slot whose count is already gone, and
/// the honest answer for "take one away from nothing" is that nothing was taken
/// and nothing reached zero.
func drop_reference(kind: ObjType, slot: Int) unsafe -> Bool {
    match kind {
        case Event -> {
            if EVENTS[slot].refs == 0 { return false }
            EVENTS[slot].refs = EVENTS[slot].refs - 1
            EVENTS[slot].refs == 0
        },
        case Waiter -> {
            if WAITERS[slot].refs == 0 { return false }
            WAITERS[slot].refs = WAITERS[slot].refs - 1
            WAITERS[slot].refs == 0
        },
        case Interrupt -> {
            if INTERRUPTS[slot].refs == 0 { return false }
            INTERRUPTS[slot].refs = INTERRUPTS[slot].refs - 1
            INTERRUPTS[slot].refs == 0
        },
        case Timer -> {
            if TIMERS[slot].refs == 0 { return false }
            TIMERS[slot].refs = TIMERS[slot].refs - 1
            TIMERS[slot].refs == 0
        },
        case Memory -> {
            if MEMORIES[slot].refs == 0 { return false }
            MEMORIES[slot].refs = MEMORIES[slot].refs - 1
            MEMORIES[slot].refs == 0
        },
        case IoMemory -> {
            if IOMEMORIES[slot].refs == 0 { return false }
            IOMEMORIES[slot].refs = IOMEMORIES[slot].refs - 1
            IOMEMORIES[slot].refs == 0
        },
        case Mapping -> {
            if MAPPINGS[slot].refs == 0 { return false }
            MAPPINGS[slot].refs = MAPPINGS[slot].refs - 1
            MAPPINGS[slot].refs == 0
        },
        // **THE ONLY TWO ARMS WHOSE ANSWER IS NOT THEIR OWN COLUMN'S ZERO**
        // (sawos design 13 D-1). A connection frees when BOTH ends have gone,
        // so each arm decrements ITS column and then asks about the pair — which
        // is what makes `unref_object`'s "zero frees" mean "both-zero frees"
        // here without that function knowing there are two kinds involved.
        //
        // **AND ONE COLUMN REACHING ZERO IS THE PEER-GONE TRANSITION**, which is
        // the one thing this function does besides count. It is here rather than
        // in `free_object` for the plainest reason: `free_object` runs only when
        // BOTH columns are zero, and peer-gone is what happens when exactly ONE
        // is — so the transition has no other site it could be written at. The
        // teardown's `unref_teardown` reaches it too, and should: a dying
        // process's endpoint going away is the same news to the far end as a
        // release of it.
        case PipeInlet -> {
            if PIPES[slot].inlet_refs == 0 { return false }
            PIPES[slot].inlet_refs = PIPES[slot].inlet_refs - 1
            // NOTHING TO DO AT THE INLET'S ZERO, and the absence is design 230's
            // close-drains-first rule made mechanical: what is staged was posted
            // by a sender that is gone, and the OUTLET may still take every word
            // of it. `Take` reads the column when the ring runs dry and answers
            // `PeerClosed` then, which is drain-then-terminal in one comparison.
            //
            // **AND A CLAIM OUTLIVES THE INLET IT WAS POSTED THROUGH** (sawos
            // design 14 D-2). A `PipeReply` is a counted object in its own right,
            // rooted in the ring slot rather than in the endpoint, so a client
            // may drop its inlet and still resolve every claim it holds — which
            // is what makes post-then-drop-the-inlet a legal shape rather than a
            // way to lose an answer.
            //
            // **WHAT IS NEW IN M4 UNIT 3 IS THAT SOMEBODY MAY BE PARKED ON THE
            // FAR END** (sawos design 15; `designs/010` ruling 2's peer-gone
            // doctrine). The OUTLET's readable level is drain-then-terminal, so
            // an inlet column reaching zero raises it — `PeerGone` once the ring
            // is dry — and a server waiting for a message that can never come
            // has to be TOLD rather than left blocked. Reentrancy: the delivery
            // can detach and unref the outlet's own attachment, which can take
            // that column to zero and free the connection under us; the
            // `pipe_frees_now` below is guarded on `PIPES[slot].state` for
            // exactly that, and its docstring already says the test is not
            // defensive.
            if PIPES[slot].inlet_refs == 0 {
                notify_if_ready(WaitableKind.PipeOutlet, slot)
            }
            pipe_frees_now(slot)
        },
        case PipeOutlet -> {
            if PIPES[slot].outlet_refs == 0 { return false }
            PIPES[slot].outlet_refs = PIPES[slot].outlet_refs - 1
            if PIPES[slot].outlet_refs == 0 {
                // **AND THE STAGED EXCHANGES ARE SETTLED** (sawos design 13 D-2,
                // as amended by design 14 D-2). Nobody can ever take again — a
                // take needs an outlet handle and this column is zero — so a
                // staged message has no future and its CLAIM is abandoned by the
                // server. `pipe_drop_staged` empties the list and gives back
                // every exchange nobody names; the ones a live claim still names
                // stay, holding the `PeerClosed` their `Resolve` is owed.
                //
                // **IT RUNS BEFORE THE NOTIFICATIONS, AND THE ORDER IS THE
                // ITERATE-WHILE-REMOVING CARE THIS UNIT OWES** (sawos design 15,
                // on `designs/010` ruling 9(a)'s census obligation). The drop
                // walks a LIST threaded through `PIPE_NEXT`, and settling an
                // exchange zeroes its link; a delivery inside that walk could
                // settle a node the walk had not reached yet and truncate it.
                // So the list walk finishes first, and the notifications go out
                // afterwards as a bounded SCAN of the ring — which needs no list
                // at all and therefore cannot be broken by what it delivers.
                pipe_drop_staged(slot)
                // The client's room-to-post level is now `PeerGone`: posting is
                // over, which is a better thing to be woken for than space.
                // **AND EVERY THREAD PARKED IN A FUSED `Call` FOR ROOM IS TOLD
                // HERE** (sawos design 17): `notify_room` serves the parked
                // callers before the subscription, and on this transition it
                // serves ALL of them, because a poster whose server end has gone
                // is waiting for something that can never happen — design 10
                // ruling 2's doctrine, reaching the one park that is on no list.
                notify_room(slot)
                // ...and every claim still naming a staged message learns that
                // its obligation can never be minted.
                notify_claims(slot)
            }
            pipe_frees_now(slot)
        },
        // **AN EXCHANGE IS THE SAME TWO-COLUMN OBJECT ONE LEVEL DOWN** (sawos
        // design 14 D-2), so these two arms read exactly like the pair above
        // them: each decrements ITS column and then answers about the WHOLE
        // exchange. `exchange_can_settle` is that question — nobody names it, and
        // nothing is owed — and it is where design 14's one asymmetry lives: a
        // STAGED message is still owed to a live outlet whatever became of its
        // claim, which is §2.1's TELL idiom.
        //
        // WHAT REACHING ZERO MEANS IS THE OTHER HALF, and it is not written here
        // because it is not a transition anybody has to record: a claim column at
        // zero IS abandoned-by-client and a request column at zero on a `Taken`
        // exchange IS abandoned-by-server, read off the counts by `Reply` and
        // `Resolve` at the moment they need an answer. Design 13's "the two
        // columns are the peer-gone state, and no flag beside them says so",
        // inherited exactly.
        //
        // **AND EACH ZERO IS NOW ALSO NEWS FOR THE OTHER HALF** (sawos design
        // 15). §2.1 ratifies both directions and unit 2 could only answer them
        // when somebody asked; a waitable pair means somebody may be PARKED, so
        // the transition is delivered here. The claim column at zero raises the
        // obligation's ABANDONED level (the server's cancellation watcher); the
        // obligation column at zero on a `Taken` exchange raises the claim's
        // reply-ready level, carrying `PeerClosed`.
        //
        // REENTRANCY, stated at the site: a reply delivery DETACHES whatever
        // mode it was attached under (ruling 11(b)), so `notify_if_ready` below
        // can re-enter this very function on the other column, settle the
        // exchange and cascade into `free_pipe`. That is safe because the
        // predicate this arm ends with reads the exchange AFTERWARDS —
        // `exchange_can_settle` answers `false` for a `Free` slot, which is the
        // honest answer for "should the caller free it" once somebody else
        // already has.
        case PipeReply -> {
            if PIPE_REPLY_REFS[slot] == 0 { return false }
            PIPE_REPLY_REFS[slot] = PIPE_REPLY_REFS[slot] - 1
            if PIPE_REPLY_REFS[slot] == 0 {
                notify_if_ready(WaitableKind.PipeRequest, slot)
            }
            exchange_can_settle(slot)
        },
        case PipeRequest -> {
            if PIPE_REQUEST_REFS[slot] == 0 { return false }
            PIPE_REQUEST_REFS[slot] = PIPE_REQUEST_REFS[slot] - 1
            if PIPE_REQUEST_REFS[slot] == 0 {
                notify_claim(slot)
            }
            exchange_can_settle(slot)
        },
        case Process -> {
            if PROCESSES[slot].refs == 0 { return false }
            PROCESSES[slot].refs = PROCESSES[slot].refs - 1
            PROCESSES[slot].refs == 0
        },
        case System -> false,
        case Clock -> false,
        case Thread -> false,
        case Unbound -> false,
    }
}

/// Give exchange `x`'s ring slot back — **and tell whoever is waiting for room
/// on the connection's client end** (design 10 ruling 8; sawos design 15).
///
/// **A SETTLE IS THE ONLY THING THAT MAKES A FULL RING NOT FULL**, so it is the
/// only place the inlet's room level can rise, and design 10 ruling 8 exists
/// precisely so a refused poster has something better than a poll loop to do
/// about it. `exchange_settle` itself lives one module down with the storage it
/// gives back, and this is the acting half wrapped around it — the same split
/// this file's header describes for every other last rite.
///
/// The `Live` test after the settle is not defensive: settling the LAST exchange
/// of a connection whose two endpoint columns are already zero frees the row
/// (`exchange_settle`'s own cascade), and a freed row has no level to raise.
func settle_exchange(x: Int) unsafe {
    let c = exchange_pipe(x)
    exchange_settle(x)
    match PIPES[c].state {
        case Free -> {},
        case Live -> { notify_room(c) },
    }
}

/// Something no longer names object `slot` of kind `kind`. **ZERO FREES.**
///
/// The ordinary decrement, and the one every op uses: `release`, `give`'s
/// caller-side unbind, `Waiter.Remove`'s detach, and the cascade out of a freed
/// Waiter. It is a plain call because the kernel runs to completion — the free
/// happens inside the syscall that dropped the last reference, so there is no
/// window in which a zero-reference object is reachable.
public(package) func unref_object(kind: ObjType, slot: Int) unsafe {
    if drop_reference(kind, slot) {
        free_object(kind, slot)
    }
}

/// The teardown's decrement: it counts, and it DOES NOT FREE.
///
/// **THE ASYMMETRY IS THE POINT, and it is what keeps design 7 D-5's authorized
/// set the size D-5 says it is.** A teardown frees what a process OWNED, by
/// ownership, in per-slab sweeps that also COUNT what they freed for the
/// report; if the close-all above those sweeps freed by reference instead,
/// every one of those counts would collapse and every teardown line in the
/// suite would move — for objects that were being reclaimed either way, in the
/// same function, microseconds apart. So the close-all keeps the ledger honest
/// (a handle in a dying table really is one reference fewer, and objects
/// OTHER processes own must see that) and the sweeps below it do the freeing
/// they have always done.
///
/// For the dying process's OWN objects the two agree by construction: the sweep
/// zeroes the slot, count included, so nothing can be left half-counted.
public(package) func unref_teardown(kind: ObjType, slot: Int) unsafe {
    let _ = drop_reference(kind, slot)
}

/// **REVOCATION: WAKE EVERY THREAD PARKED ON WAITER `w`, WITH `Revoked`**
/// (sawos design 12, M4 unit 0; `designs/010` ruling 1). The second of the
/// Waiter's two last rites: the cascade above it releases what this Waiter was
/// WATCHING, and this answers everybody who was watching THROUGH it.
///
/// **NO RECORD IS WRITTEN, and that is the whole difference from a delivery.**
/// `wake_one_waiter` answers a parked `Wait` by copying a wait record into the
/// caller's memory and THEN writing `Ok` into its frame, because something
/// became ready and the record is what became ready. Nothing became ready here:
/// there is no key, because the key names an attachment and the attachment list
/// is going; no tag, because no waitable fired; no payload, because there is no
/// value. So a revocation writes the STATUS REGISTER and nothing else, and the
/// woken `wait()` returns `Err(Revoked)` with the caller's buffer exactly as it
/// left it. Said as the pair it is: A DELIVERY COPIES OUT AND THEN ANSWERS `Ok`;
/// A REVOCATION ANSWERS `Revoked` AND COPIES NOTHING.
///
/// **THAT IS ALSO WHY THIS LIVES HERE AND NOT IN `wake`.** Delivery sits above
/// the process teardown because it goes through `copy_out` — writing a process's
/// memory is what fixes its altitude — and `refs` sits below. A revocation
/// touches no process memory, so it needs neither the copy door nor that
/// altitude: it needs a frame's status word and the ready queue, both of which
/// are `threads`', below this file. The property that makes it not-a-delivery is
/// exactly the property that lets it live beside the free it belongs to.
///
/// **THE WALK IS THE WHOLE LIST, not a pop.** §2.2 lets several threads park on
/// one Waiter, and a distribution is what a READINESS is — one ready handle
/// feeds one waiter. A revocation is not a readiness: the thing every one of
/// them is waiting on has ceased to exist, so every one of them is answered.
///
/// **IT IS PROCESS-BLIND, like every wake.** Whose threads are on the list is
/// not this function's question — a Waiter handle can be minted, and a Waiter's
/// blocked list is simply the threads that parked on it.
///
/// `wait_buf` is cleared because it is a promise this wake does not keep: it
/// records where a record was going to go, and no record is going there.
func revoke_blocked(w: Int) unsafe {
    var cur = WAITERS[w].blocked
    WAITERS[w].blocked = 0
    while cur != 0 {
        let slot = cur - 1
        // Read the link BEFORE `ready_push` takes the field for the ready
        // queue — a thread is on at most one list at a time, and this is the
        // moment it changes lists.
        cur = THREADS[slot].link
        THREADS[slot].wait_buf = 0
        write_result(frame_slot(slot), op_status(SosStatus.Revoked))
        THREADS[slot].state = ThreadState.Runnable
        ready_push(slot)
    }
}

/// Give object `slot` of kind `kind` back to its slab, and run the kind's own
/// last rites.
///
/// **THE LAST RITES ARE WHAT MAKE THIS A MATRIX AND NOT A LOOP.** Three kinds
/// have something to say to the machine or to the rest of the kernel on the way
/// out, and the reasons are the teardown's own, arriving one object at a time
/// instead of one process at a time:
///
///   - an INTERRUPT MASKS ITS LINE. A device the dropping process was driving
///     may still be asserting, and an unmasked line with no object behind it
///     would be taken, reported and masked once per fire forever. Masking is
///     the kernel taking the line back — §9's handover run backwards — and it
///     is what makes the line BINDABLE AGAIN, mid-life, which no release could
///     achieve before this unit.
///   - a TIMER REPROGRAMS THE COMPARATOR. A dropped armed deadline is one the
///     kernel would otherwise keep waking itself for with nobody left to tell.
///   - a WAITER DETACHES ITS LIST, which is the one cascade in the system —
///     **AND WAKES WHAT IS PARKED ON IT** (sawos design 12, M4 unit 0). It is
///     the one kind with two things to say, and they are said to opposite
///     sides: the detach releases what this Waiter was WATCHING, the revocation
///     answers everybody who was watching THROUGH it.
///
/// And one kind has something deliberate NOT to do — see the `Mapping` arm.
///
/// THE QUOTA IS CREDITED AGAINST THE SLOT'S OWN `process` FIELD, which is
/// creator-pays (D-3): whoever was charged for the object is who gets it back,
/// whichever process happened to drop the last handle. `Mapping` and `Process`
/// are the two arms with no credit here, each for its own stated reason.
public(package) func free_object(kind: ObjType, slot: Int) unsafe {
    match kind {
        case Event -> {
            // AN ATTACHED EVENT CANNOT BE HERE — an attachment is a counted
            // reference (D-1's table), so reaching zero means nothing is
            // watching. The test is the invariant written down: if it ever
            // fired, the count and the wait machinery would have disagreed.
            if EVENTS[slot].attachment != 0 {
                fatal_kernel("an event reached zero references while attached",
                             slot as UInt)
            }
            let owner = EVENTS[slot].process
            EVENTS[slot] = EventSlot(state: SlotState.Free, process: 0,
                                     mode: EventMode.Or, word: 0,
                                     attachment: 0, refs: 0)
            quota_credit(owner, QuotaKind.Event)
        },
        case Waiter -> {
            // **THE ONE CASCADE.** A Waiter's attachments are references it
            // HOLDS, so its death releases them — and each release may take a
            // waitable to zero, which frees and credits through this same
            // function. The list is walked whole rather than through
            // `detach_attachment`, because there is no list left to unlink
            // from: the Waiter is going.
            var cur = WAITERS[slot].attached
            while cur != 0 {
                let a = cur - 1
                let next = ATTACHMENTS[a].link
                let watched = ATTACHMENTS[a].kind
                let target = ATTACHMENTS[a].target
                ATTACHMENTS[a] = Attachment(state: SlotState.Free,
                                            kind: WaitableKind.Event, target: 0,
                                            waiter: 0, key: 0, oneshot: false,
                                            link: 0)
                set_waitable_attachment(watched, target, 0)
                unref_waitable(watched, target)
                cur = next
            }

            // **AND EVERY THREAD PARKED ON IT IS WOKEN, WITH `Revoked`** (sawos
            // design 12, M4 unit 0; `designs/010` rulings 1 and 2 — THE
            // PEER-GONE DOCTRINE, of which this is the first instance).
            // NOTHING PARKED CAN BE SILENTLY DOOMED: what these threads were
            // waiting on has ceased to exist, so they are TOLD, in the status
            // register of the very syscall that parked them, and
            // `revoke_blocked`'s docstring carries the no-record contrast that
            // makes this a revocation rather than a delivery.
            //
            // It supersedes the recorded "legal-but-doomed" stance this arm
            // carried through M3 — a parked thread left `Blocked` forever for
            // the deadlock report to name. The alternative the ruling weighed
            // and REJECTED was a husk: count a parked thread as a reference so
            // the Waiter never reaches zero. That keeps the thread alive and
            // makes the drop SILENT, which turns an error a program can handle
            // into a hang somebody has to infer.
            //
            // THE COUNT IS THE GATE, NOT THE RELEASE, and that distinction is
            // what makes this revocation rather than any-release: a release
            // with a minted sibling still live never reaches this function at
            // all, so it frees nothing and wakes nobody. Only the LAST name
            // going is the object ceasing to exist, and only that is news.
            //
            // It runs AFTER the cascade and BEFORE the slot clears, which is
            // free of consequence in both directions — the cascade reads no
            // thread and the wake reads no attachment — and reads in the order
            // the arm is about: release what this Waiter held, answer who held
            // it, then hand the slot back.
            revoke_blocked(slot)

            let owner = WAITERS[slot].process
            // A REUSED SLOT STARTS WITH AN EMPTY BLOCKED LIST, which is what
            // this literal says and what keeps the next Waiter in this slot
            // from inheriting anybody. It was the half of the old stranding
            // paragraph that was about the SLOT rather than about the thread,
            // and it survives the ruling intact — the wake above is what makes
            // the list empty honestly rather than merely emptily.
            WAITERS[slot] = WaiterSlot(state: SlotState.Free, process: 0,
                                       attached: 0, blocked: 0, refs: 0)
            quota_credit(owner, QuotaKind.Waiter)
        },
        case Interrupt -> {
            if INTERRUPTS[slot].attachment != 0 {
                fatal_kernel("an interrupt reached zero references while attached",
                             slot as UInt)
            }
            let owner = INTERRUPTS[slot].process
            hal.irq_mask(INTERRUPTS[slot].line)
            INTERRUPTS[slot] = InterruptSlot(state: SlotState.Free, process: 0,
                                             line: 0, pending: false,
                                             attachment: 0, refs: 0)
            quota_credit(owner, QuotaKind.Interrupt)
        },
        case Timer -> {
            if TIMERS[slot].attachment != 0 {
                fatal_kernel("a timer reached zero references while attached",
                             slot as UInt)
            }
            let owner = TIMERS[slot].process
            TIMERS[slot] = TimerSlot(state: SlotState.Free, process: 0, clock: 0,
                                     armed: false, deadline_ns: 0,
                                     interval_ns: 0, fires: 0, attachment: 0,
                                     refs: 0)
            reprogram_timer()
            quota_credit(owner, QuotaKind.Timer)
        },
        case Memory -> {
            // THE SLOT COMES BACK; THE RANGE GOES NOWHERE. A front-cut parent
            // cannot absorb an arbitrary hole, so the bytes stay spent until
            // an allocator exists to spend them again (design 6 D-2; §2.5's
            // narrowing).
            let owner = MEMORIES[slot].process
            MEMORIES[slot] = MemorySlot(state: SlotState.Free, process: 0,
                                        base: 0, len: 0, refs: 0)
            quota_credit(owner, QuotaKind.Memory)
        },
        case IoMemory -> {
            // MMIO IS PINNED, so this touches no hardware whatever: the
            // registers were there before the window and will be there after
            // it. What actually revokes a driver's reach is the ROW, and a row
            // is a Mapping.
            let owner = IOMEMORIES[slot].process
            IOMEMORIES[slot] = IoMemorySlot(state: SlotState.Free, process: 0,
                                            base: 0, len: 0, refs: 0)
            quota_credit(owner, QuotaKind.IoMemory)
        },
        case Mapping -> {
            // **THE SLOT FREES AND THE ROW STAYS** (sawos design 7 D-2), and
            // this arm is §2.5's own sentence made mechanical rather than an
            // omission. A Mapping OWNS its row; the row does not own the
            // Mapping. So a mapped-but-unreferenced Mapping — every handle
            // released, the grant still installed — is exactly §2.5's
            // "dropped without unmap = permanent, safe-but-leaked", and the row
            // becomes unremovable until the TARGET's teardown, which is what
            // "leaked" has always meant. Removing it here would be a
            // revocation nobody asked for: the process whose domain carries it
            // is a different process from the one that dropped the handle.
            //
            // NO QUOTA IS CREDITED HERE, and that is the consequence of the row
            // being what the mapping quota counts (see `QuotaKind.Mapping`).
            // The charge follows the ROW: `Unmap` credits it, the target's
            // teardown zeroes it, and a leaked row stays charged for exactly as
            // long as it stays installed — which is what keeps `used` and
            // `grant_count` in step and `install_row`'s `fatal` unreachable.
            MAPPINGS[slot] = MappingSlot(state: SlotState.Free, process: 0,
                                         target: 0, row: NO_ROW, refs: 0)
        },
        case Process -> {
            // A PROCESS IS A WAITABLE SINCE M3 UNIT 5.5, so it joins the three
            // arms above in asserting the invariant an attachment-as-reference
            // buys (sawos design 8): reaching zero means nothing is watching,
            // because a watcher IS a reference. The test is the invariant
            // written down, and it is what makes attach-after-death sound
            // against the reclaim below — if it ever fired, a supervisor would
            // be parked on a slot the kernel had already given away.
            if PROCESSES[slot].attachment != 0 {
                fatal_kernel("a process reached zero references while attached",
                             slot as UInt)
            }
            // A LIVE PROCESS IS NOT FREED BY LOSING ITS LAST HANDLE — it is
            // still running, and its slot is what it is running in. Only a
            // `Gone` slot's last reader going makes the §8 status word
            // unreadable, and `reclaim_process_slot` is that rule, unchanged
            // from design 3 D-3 except that it now READS the count this unit
            // maintains instead of walking every table to recompute it.
            //
            // The ledger is not credited either: the teardown already zeroed
            // this slot's rows, and a process is not something another process
            // is charged for.
            reclaim_process_slot(slot)
        },
        // **BOTH ENDS ARE GONE, SO THE CONNECTION IS** (sawos design 13 D-1).
        // The two arms are one act written twice, and they have to be: the
        // matrix is over `ObjType`, and reaching this function through EITHER
        // kind means `drop_reference` already found both columns at zero. Which
        // end happened to be released last is not a fact anything downstream
        // has any use for.
        //
        // The staging needs nothing said to it here. Either the outlet went
        // first and `drop_reference` dropped what was staged at that transition,
        // or the inlet went first and the outlet drained (or declined to) before
        // its own last handle went — and in both cases the ring is plain storage
        // a later `pipe_create` overwrites. Bytes are not a resource this slab
        // hands back.
        case PipeInlet -> { free_pipe(slot) },
        case PipeOutlet -> { free_pipe(slot) },
        // **AND THE ONE-SHOT PAIR IS THE SAME ACT WRITTEN TWICE AGAIN** (sawos
        // design 14 D-1/D-2). Reaching this function through EITHER one-shot kind
        // means `drop_reference` already found both of the exchange's columns at
        // zero with nothing owed, so which half happened to go last is not a fact
        // anything downstream has a use for.
        //
        // NO QUOTA IS CREDITED HERE, and that is D-1's ruling rather than an
        // omission: a claim mints NO quota row. The scarce thing an exchange
        // occupies is a ring slot, and the ring is part of the CONNECTION —
        // charged once at `PipeCreate`, credited once when the row goes back. A
        // per-exchange row would charge a client twice for the same kilobytes and
        // would make design 10 ruling 4's "zero one-shot quota rows on the fast
        // path" a difference in kind rather than one of handle traffic.
        //
        // The settle may CASCADE into freeing the connection — the ring slot is
        // storage inside the `PIPES` row — and that cascade terminates because an
        // exchange references its connection and a connection references no
        // exchange.
        case PipeReply -> { settle_exchange(slot) },
        case PipeRequest -> { settle_exchange(slot) },
        case System -> {
            fatal_kernel("free_object on the System singleton", slot as UInt)
        },
        case Clock -> {
            fatal_kernel("free_object on a kernel-eternal Clock", slot as UInt)
        },
        case Thread -> {
            fatal_kernel("free_object on a Thread — the join protocol owns it",
                         slot as UInt)
        },
        case Unbound -> {
            fatal_kernel("free_object on a handle that names nothing",
                         slot as UInt)
        },
    }
}

// =============================================================================
// THE TEARDOWN'S WRITE-OFF — what replaced the by-charged-process force-free
// (spec §2.1; `designs/010` ruling 10, landed by sawos design 14 D-3)
// =============================================================================
//
// **REFERENCES GOVERN LIFETIME, SO A TEARDOWN NEVER FORCE-FREES A COUNTED
// KIND.** `end_process` used to sweep the Memory, IoMemory and Pipe slabs by
// `process == p` and zero every slot the dying process had been CHARGED for —
// a pre-refcount survivor, from the units that had no count to consult — and
// design 13's finding 2 named the hazard it leaves: a connection whose creator
// dies while a PEER still holds an end has its row freed under that holder,
// staged messages and all. Ruling 10 takes the fix that deletes bookkeeping
// rather than adding it. A slot outlives its creator for exactly as long as
// anybody holds a reference; possession keeps alive, which is the capability
// model's own answer.
//
// **WHAT IS LEFT IS NOT A SWEEP BY OWNERSHIP BUT A REAP OF WHAT NOBODY NAMES**,
// and the reason it cannot simply be deleted is design 7 D-5's deliberate
// asymmetry one function up: the close-all counts WITHOUT freeing
// (`unref_teardown`), because the per-slab sweeps that DO free are also what
// count for the teardown report. Delete the three silent sweeps outright and a
// slot the dying process held the last handle onto reaches zero references with
// nothing left to free it — a leak, not a write-off. So the CONDITION changes
// from "charged to this process" to "named by nobody", which is ruling 10's
// sentence made mechanical: the force-free is gone, and the reclamation the
// close-all defers still happens.
//
// It frees through `free_object`, so every kind's own last rites and its quota
// CREDIT run exactly as they do on the ordinary release path — which is the
// other half of what the old sweeps got wrong. They zeroed a slot another
// process had been charged for and credited nobody.
//
// **THE OTHER FIVE SWEEPS STAY, and Mapping stays with them.** Threads, Events,
// Waiters, Interrupts and Timers are counted for the teardown report, and D-5
// authorises no change to those numbers. A Mapping is a counted kind whose sweep
// looks like the three deleted ones and is NOT one: `mapping_rights()` withholds
// `Transfer`, so a Mapping handle cannot leave the process that owns it and a
// sibling cannot outlive its owner — the hazard ruling 10 names cannot arise —
// and its second arm (clearing the row of a mapping whose TARGET died) is not a
// free at all.

/// Reap every Memory, IoMemory, exchange and Pipe connection that NOTHING NAMES
/// any more. Called by `end_process` after the close-all.
///
/// The order is inner-to-outer: an exchange lives inside a connection's row, so
/// the exchanges are given back first and the connections they were in can then
/// answer `pipe_idle` honestly. (`exchange_settle` cascades into `free_pipe`
/// itself, so the second loop is what catches a connection with no exchanges at
/// all — the ordinary case.)
public(package) func reap_unreferenced() unsafe {
    var m: Int = 0
    while m < MAX_MEMORIES {
        match MEMORIES[m].state {
            case Free -> {},
            case Live -> {
                if MEMORIES[m].refs == 0 { free_object(ObjType.Memory, m) }
            },
        }
        m = m + 1
    }

    var io: Int = 0
    while io < MAX_IOMEMORIES {
        match IOMEMORIES[io].state {
            case Free -> {},
            case Live -> {
                if IOMEMORIES[io].refs == 0 { free_object(ObjType.IoMemory, io) }
            },
        }
        io = io + 1
    }

    var x: Int = 0
    while x < PIPE_EXCHANGES {
        if exchange_can_settle(x) { exchange_settle(x) }
        x = x + 1
    }

    var c: Int = 0
    while c < MAX_PIPES {
        if pipe_frees_now(c) { free_pipe(c) }
        c = c + 1
    }
}

/// The wait machinery's kinds, translated into the object kinds this matrix is
/// written over.
///
/// A SECOND ENUM MEETS THE FIRST HERE, and nowhere else. `WaitableKind` exists
/// because attaching also spends a right and which enum's bits that is depends
/// on the kind (`waitables`' header); `ObjType` is what a handle entry carries.
/// One translation written out beats a cast between two enums that happen to be
/// numbered differently, and it is total, so the third waitable kind cost it
/// one line — and so did the FOURTH (sawos design 8), which is where the two
/// enums genuinely stop being numbered alike: `WaitableKind.Process` is 3 and
/// `ObjType.Process` is not.
func waitable_obj_type(kind: WaitableKind) -> ObjType {
    match kind {
        case Event -> ObjType.Event,
        case Interrupt -> ObjType.Interrupt,
        case Timer -> ObjType.Timer,
        case Process -> ObjType.Process,
        // **AND THE FOUR PIPE KINDS COST IT FOUR LINES** (sawos design 15),
        // which is what the translation being TOTAL is for: the two enums are
        // numbered differently on purpose — `WaitableKind` names only what can
        // be attached — and a cast between them would have been a coincidence
        // that stopped being true at exactly this unit.
        case PipeOutlet -> ObjType.PipeOutlet,
        case PipeInlet -> ObjType.PipeInlet,
        case PipeReply -> ObjType.PipeReply,
        case PipeRequest -> ObjType.PipeRequest,
    }
}

/// An attachment now names this waitable (§2.2; sawos design 7 D-1).
public(package) func ref_waitable(kind: WaitableKind, slot: Int) unsafe {
    ref_object(waitable_obj_type(kind), slot)
}

/// An attachment no longer names this waitable. Zero frees, as everywhere.
public(package) func unref_waitable(kind: WaitableKind, slot: Int) unsafe {
    unref_object(waitable_obj_type(kind), slot)
}

/// The TEARDOWN's version: it counts, and it DOES NOT FREE — `unref_teardown`
/// reached through the wait machinery's own enum.
///
/// **IT EXISTS BECAUSE M3 UNIT 5.5 MADE AN ATTACHMENT ABLE TO CROSS PROCESSES**
/// (sawos design 8). Until a Process was waitable, every attachment joined a
/// Waiter and a waitable of the SAME process, so the teardown's attachment
/// sweep could free both ends and be done; a supervisor's attachment onto a
/// CHILD's Process slot is the first pair that spans two processes, and the
/// dying supervisor's side owes the other side a decrement exactly as the
/// close-all above it owes one for a handle onto somebody else's object. Same
/// asymmetry, same reason: the ledger stays honest and the per-slab sweeps keep
/// doing the freeing they have always done.
public(package) func unref_waitable_teardown(kind: WaitableKind, slot: Int) unsafe {
    unref_teardown(waitable_obj_type(kind), slot)
}

// =============================================================================
// DELIVERY — readiness turning into a woken thread (spec §2.2), and the
// attachment lifetimes that ride on it (`designs/010` rulings 9 and 11(b))
// =============================================================================
//
// **THIS WAS `kcore.wake` UNTIL M4 UNIT 3** (sawos design 15), and the file
// header carries the argument for why it is here instead: a delivery drops a
// reference and a dropped reference delivers, so the two are one act and a
// module boundary between them would be the cycle DF-232e does not diagnose.
//
// Three funnels, one act, exactly as before: `notify_ready` is the only place a
// ready waitable reaches its Waiter, `wake_one_waiter` hands the answer to at
// most one parked thread, and `deliver_attachment` is where the answer is READ,
// the READINESS SPENT and — since ruling 9(b) — THE ATTACHMENT RELEASED, all in
// one step, so no kind is drained on one path and not the other.

/// The record `Waiter.Wait` answers with, in kernel memory (spec §2.2, as
/// amended by design 178 unit 3 rider 3 and grown a fourth word by
/// `designs/010` ruling 11(a)).
///
/// Declaration order IS the layout (design 58), so the field names here and the
/// word indices `sosabi` publishes are one description — pinned by the assert
/// below rather than by agreement. **THE BODY IS NOT A FIELD**: it is a separate
/// copy at `wait_header_bytes()` into the same caller buffer, out of the ring
/// slot it already lives in, which is what makes the copy sized to the DELIVERY
/// instead of to the record.
struct WaitRecord {
    key: UInt,
    tag: UInt,
    payload: UInt,
    body_len: UInt,
}

static_assert(sizeof<WaitRecord>() == WAIT_HEADER_WORDS * sizeof<UInt>(),
              "the wait record header must be exactly the words sosabi publishes")

/// Build one and hand it to a process. The only producer of the wire form.
///
/// A REFUSED DESTINATION HERE IS A KERNEL BUG, NOT A PROCESS ERROR, and that is
/// why it stops the machine rather than terminating anybody. `Waiter.Wait`
/// validates the buffer AT THE CALL — that is exactly what `copy_out_check`
/// exists for and what `ThreadSlot.wait_buf`'s docstring records — and a
/// process's writable window is fixed for its life, so a buffer that passed
/// then cannot fail now. Reaching either arm means the two facts disagree, which
/// is a kernel invariant broken and nothing a process could have done. **BOTH
/// COPIES ARE INSIDE THE ONE CHECKED WINDOW**: the check was for
/// `wait_record_bytes()`, which is the header plus a whole message body, and
/// `body_len` can never exceed `PIPE_BODY_BYTES`.
///
/// It is also the shape this code's ALTITUDE forces (sawos design 2): the wake
/// path runs below the scheduler — a timer expiry reaches here out of the IRQ
/// path with no current thread of its own — so terminating a process from here
/// would be terminating one with nowhere to go next. The door reports, and the
/// callers that can kill are the ones above the switch point.
func deliver_wait_record(p: Int, dst: UInt, key: UInt,
                         answer: &WaitAnswer) unsafe {
    var rec = WaitRecord(key: key, tag: answer.tag as UInt,
                         payload: answer.payload, body_len: answer.body_len)
    if let why = copy_out(p, dst, ((&var rec) as UnsafePointer<WaitRecord>) as UInt,
                          wait_header_bytes() as UInt) {
        fatal("wait record refused by the copy door", (why as UInt8) as UInt,
              dst, p as UInt, ExitCode.ProcessFault)
    }
    // **THE SECOND COPY IS THE WHOLE OF RULING 11(a)** — and it does not happen
    // at all for the seven kinds whose answer is a word. A delivery that carries
    // bytes carries them out of the ring slot that already holds them, so the
    // reply never transits a kernel buffer and the record's growth costs a
    // Timer waiter nothing but address space.
    if answer.body_len != 0 {
        if let why = copy_out(p, dst + (wait_header_bytes() as UInt),
                              answer.body_src, answer.body_len) {
            fatal("wait record body refused by the copy door",
                  (why as UInt8) as UInt, dst, p as UInt, ExitCode.ProcessFault)
        }
    }
}

/// **DOES A DELIVERY OF THIS KIND CONSUME THE SUBSCRIPTION, whatever mode it was
/// attached under?** (`designs/010` ruling 11(b)).
///
/// ONE KIND SAYS YES, and it says yes because of what its delivery IS: a reply
/// is a ONE-SHOT, the record carries the answer, and the exchange is discharged
/// by the copy-out — so by ruling 9(a)'s own logic (an act that consumes the
/// object detaches it) the attachment goes with it. A persistent reading would
/// be a subscription to an event that has already happened for the last time.
///
/// Every other kind is the attacher's choice, which is `Attachment.oneshot`.
/// Written as an exhaustive match rather than a `kind == PipeReply` so that the
/// next one-shot waitable has to answer here too.
func delivery_consumes(kind: WaitableKind) -> Bool {
    match kind {
        case Event -> false,
        case Interrupt -> false,
        case Timer -> false,
        case Process -> false,
        case PipeOutlet -> false,
        case PipeInlet -> false,
        case PipeReply -> true,
        case PipeRequest -> false,
    }
}

/// ANSWER ONE ATTACHMENT INTO A PROCESS'S MEMORY, spend what the answer spent,
/// and release the subscription if this delivery ended it. Its entry points,
/// both of them:
///
///   - `waiter_wait`, when something was ALREADY ready and nothing parks;
///   - `wake_one_waiter`, handing a ready attachment to a thread that did.
///
/// A funnel rather than two call sites, because "read the answer" and "record
/// that it was delivered" have to be one step: split apart, a kind whose
/// readiness is CONSUMED by delivery — the Timer and, since Aug 17, the Event —
/// would be drained on one path and not the other, and the bug would be a
/// periodic timer that reports the same fire twice to whichever caller forgot.
/// Both paths are equally "a record reached a thread", which is exactly the
/// event `waitable_consume` is about.
///
/// THE READ AND THE CLEAR ARE ONE ACT, and this function's body is where that is
/// true: `waitable_answer` takes the value, `waitable_consume` clears it, and
/// nothing between them can run. The kernel takes no trap in kernel mode (design
/// 178 D2 — interrupts are taken from user mode only), so a signal cannot land
/// inside this window; one landing after it finds the object empty and re-arms
/// readiness with its own bits alone, which is neither a loss nor a double
/// count.
///
/// **AND THE DETACH IS THE THIRD PART, ADDED BY M4 UNIT 3** (`designs/010`
/// rulings 9(b) and 11(b)). An attachment ends here when the attacher asked for
/// one delivery (`AttachMode.OneShot`) or when the KIND's delivery is inherently
/// consuming — and because an attachment is a counted reference, ending it drops
/// one. **THAT DROP CAN CASCADE ALL THE WAY TO A FREED CONNECTION**: a reply
/// delivered to a consuming attachment takes the exchange's claim column to
/// zero, which settles the ring slot, which may free the `PIPES` row it lives
/// in. Nothing above this line reads any of that afterwards — the answer, the
/// key and the waiter were all taken before the detach, and `wake_one_waiter`
/// below touches only the THREAD — which is the reentrancy argument this site
/// owes.
///
/// It is also why the read of `ATTACHMENTS[a]` happens up front: after the
/// detach that slot is `Free` and belongs to whoever allocates next.
public(package) func deliver_attachment(p: Int, dst: UInt, a: Int) unsafe {
    let kind = ATTACHMENTS[a].kind
    let target = ATTACHMENTS[a].target
    let key = ATTACHMENTS[a].key
    let waiter = ATTACHMENTS[a].waiter
    let ends = ATTACHMENTS[a].oneshot || delivery_consumes(kind)

    let answer = waitable_answer(kind, target)
    deliver_wait_record(p, dst, key, &answer)
    waitable_consume(kind, target)

    if ends {
        detach_attachment(waiter, a)
        unref_waitable(kind, target)
    }
}

/// Hand attachment `a` to ONE thread parked on `w`, if any is (§2.2).
///
/// The record goes into the woken thread's own buffer and its STATUS into the
/// woken thread's frame, both HERE and nowhere else — the same discipline
/// `exit_thread` follows for a joiner: a thread that blocked in a syscall was
/// answered by nobody when it blocked, so its waker is its single writer.
///
/// THE WRITER IS A DIFFERENT THREAD from the one being answered, which is sound
/// today because M2 has one address space; `copy_out`'s docstring is where that
/// stops being free. Since the Interrupt object landed the writer need not be a
/// THREAD at all — the trap path and the idle poll both reach here — which
/// changes nothing about the write and everything about why the check that
/// validated the buffer had to happen back at the `Wait` call.
///
/// **THE THREAD LEAVES THE BLOCKED LIST BEFORE THE DELIVERY**, which is what
/// keeps M4 unit 3's cascades away from that list: a delivery can now free
/// objects and even a whole connection, and none of it can reach a walk this
/// function has already finished.
public(package) func wake_one_waiter(w: Int, a: Int) unsafe {
    let head = WAITERS[w].blocked
    if head == 0 {
        return
    }
    let slot = head - 1
    WAITERS[w].blocked = THREADS[slot].link
    THREADS[slot].link = 0

    deliver_attachment(THREADS[slot].process, THREADS[slot].wait_buf, a)
    THREADS[slot].wait_buf = 0
    write_result(frame_slot(slot), op_ok())
    THREADS[slot].state = ThreadState.Runnable
    ready_push(slot)
}

/// A waitable just became ready: tell its Waiter, if it has one.
///
/// THE ONE PLACE READINESS TURNS INTO A WAKE, reached from every producer there
/// is — an Event's `Signal`, an Interrupt's line firing, a Timer's expiry, a
/// process ending, and since M4 unit 3 a pipe's four levels. None of them knows
/// anything about waiters beyond the attachment field the matrix reads.
public(package) func notify_ready(kind: WaitableKind, slot: Int) unsafe {
    let a = waitable_attachment(kind, slot)
    if a == 0 {
        return
    }
    wake_one_waiter(ATTACHMENTS[a - 1].waiter, a - 1)
}

/// `notify_ready` for a caller that has NOT established readiness — ask first.
///
/// **THE PEER-GONE SITES ARE WHY IT EXISTS** (sawos design 15). Every other
/// producer knows it just made something ready: a signal wrote a word, a line
/// fired, a deadline passed. A reference reaching zero is different — it raises
/// a level that may ALREADY have been raised (a readable outlet with messages
/// still staged), or may not be raised at all (an obligation column falling on
/// an exchange whose reply was already delivered) — so the transition is asked
/// about rather than asserted. Cheap: the attachment field is one load and
/// answers "nobody is watching" for almost every call.
public(package) func notify_if_ready(kind: WaitableKind, slot: Int) unsafe {
    if waitable_attachment(kind, slot) == 0 {
        return
    }
    if not waitable_ready(kind, slot) {
        return
    }
    notify_ready(kind, slot)
}

/// Tell every watched CLAIM in connection `c` that its level just changed.
///
/// **A SCAN AND NOT A LIST WALK, deliberately** (`designs/010` ruling 9(a)'s
/// iterate-while-removing census). It is called at the outlet's zero-arm, where
/// each delivery it makes can settle the very exchange it just visited and can
/// cascade into freeing the connection — so it must not be holding a position in
/// any structure those acts edit. A bounded walk over the ring's fixed indices
/// holds nothing: an exchange settled underneath it becomes `Free`, which is
/// unattached, which the next iteration skips. `PIPE_INFLIGHT` is 16 and
/// `kcore.limits`' audit note already covers a scan of it.
func notify_claims(c: Int) unsafe {
    var i: Int = 0
    while i < PIPE_INFLIGHT {
        notify_claim(pipe_exchange_at(c, i))
        i = i + 1
    }
}

// =============================================================================
// THE FUSED CALL'S WAKE ARM (sawos design 17, M4 unit 3.5; `designs/010` ruling
// 4's Aug-31 rider)
// =============================================================================
//
// **ONE ARM, ASKED AT ONE QUESTION: is this claim a PARKED THREAD's, or a
// SUBSCRIPTION's?** The rider says it in as many words — "the wake dispatch
// grows ONE arm (a claim is a parked fused call -> wake the thread, or a
// split-phase claim -> notify the Waiter — not a second wake protocol)" — and
// `notify_claim` below is that sentence. Every producer of reply-readiness now
// calls it instead of `notify_if_ready(PipeReply, …)`, so there is exactly one
// place in the kernel where the two shapes of client are told apart.
//
// **THE SECOND ARM IS THE ROOM LEVEL'S, AND IT IS THE SAME SHAPE.** `Call` never
// answers `WouldBlock` (design 10 ruling 8, reached from inside the op), so a
// full ring parks the caller — and the level that ends that park is the inlet's
// room-to-post one, which already had exactly two producers. `notify_room`
// replaces `notify_if_ready(PipeInlet, …)` at both of them, asks the same
// question, and routes into the same resume.
//
// WHAT A THREAD-TIED CLAIM CANNOT BE IS ATTACHED. A fused call mints no
// `PipeReply` handle at all, so there is no word anybody could pass to
// `Waiter.Add` — `PIPE_REPLY_ATTACH[x]` is provably zero for as long as
// `PIPE_CALLER[x]` is not, which is why the two arms are alternatives rather
// than two things to do in sequence.

/// **A CLAIM'S LEVEL CHANGED — tell whoever holds it, whichever kind of holder
/// that is.** The one arm.
public(package) func notify_claim(x: Int) unsafe {
    let parked = PIPE_CALLER[x]
    if parked == 0 {
        notify_if_ready(WaitableKind.PipeReply, x)
        return
    }
    if not waitable_ready(WaitableKind.PipeReply, x) {
        return
    }
    wake_call_reply(parked - 1, x)
}

/// **ANSWER A THREAD PARKED IN A FUSED `Call`** — `deliver_attachment`'s
/// opposite number, and deliberately its mirror image.
///
/// The two do the same three things in the same order and differ only in where
/// the answer goes: a delivery writes a wait RECORD and a fused call writes the
/// reply BYTES and a value register, because a `Call` resumes inside its own
/// syscall rather than inside a `Wait`. So the readiness is read through
/// `waitable_answer` — the same matrix arm the split-phase path reads — the
/// exchange is spent through `waitable_consume`, and the claim's reference is
/// dropped last.
///
/// **THE UNREF IS LAST, AND THAT ORDER IS THE ONE SAFE ONE.** It can settle the
/// ring slot and cascade all the way to a freed connection, and `exchange_settle`
/// asserts that nobody is parked — so the tie has to be gone before the
/// reference is. Nothing after the unref reads the exchange.
///
/// A REFUSED COPY-OUT IS A KERNEL BUG, on `deliver_wait_record`'s exact terms:
/// `pipe_call` validated the reply buffer while the caller was running, and a
/// process's window is fixed for its life.
func wake_call_reply(t: Int, x: Int) unsafe {
    let p = THREADS[t].process
    let dst = THREADS[t].wait_buf
    let answer = waitable_answer(WaitableKind.PipeReply, x)
    var result = op_status(SosStatus.PeerClosed)
    if answer.payload == (ReplyLevel.Data as UInt) {
        if answer.body_len != 0 {
            if let why = copy_out(p, dst, answer.body_src, answer.body_len) {
                fatal("fused call reply refused by the copy door",
                      (why as UInt8) as UInt, dst, p as UInt,
                      ExitCode.ProcessFault)
            }
        }
        result = op_value(answer.body_len)
    }
    waitable_consume(WaitableKind.PipeReply, x)
    clear_pending_call(t)
    write_result(frame_slot(t), result)
    THREADS[t].state = ThreadState.Runnable
    ready_push(t)
    unref_waitable(WaitableKind.PipeReply, x)
}

/// **A CONNECTION'S ROOM LEVEL CHANGED — serve the parked callers, then the
/// subscription.** The second arm.
///
/// It LOOPS where `notify_claim` does not, and the asymmetry is the two levels'
/// own: a claim is one exchange with at most one holder, while room is a
/// connection-wide level several threads may be waiting on at once. A settle
/// frees ONE slot, so the ordinary pass serves one caller and the next finds no
/// room and stops; a PEER-GONE transition serves every one of them, which is
/// design 10 ruling 2's doctrine — nothing parked can be silently doomed, and a
/// poster whose server end has died is exactly a thread waiting for something
/// that can no longer happen.
///
/// The subscription is told afterwards and asks its own question: if a resumed
/// caller took the freed slot, `notify_if_ready` finds the level false and says
/// nothing, which is the honest answer rather than a special case.
public(package) func notify_room(c: Int) unsafe {
    var t = call_room_waiter(c)
    while t != NO_THREAD {
        if not resume_call_room(t, c) {
            break
        }
        t = call_room_waiter(c)
    }
    notify_if_ready(WaitableKind.PipeInlet, c)
}

/// Stage a room-parked call's message now that there may be somewhere to put it,
/// and re-park it on its exchange. Answers whether the thread stopped waiting
/// for room — `false` means the ring is still full and it is still parked, which
/// is what stops `notify_room`'s loop.
///
/// **IT IS `pipe_post`'s BODY, RUN IN SOMEBODY ELSE'S CONTEXT**, and the two
/// differences are both consequences of that. The questions are asked in
/// `pipe_post`'s own order (the outlet column before the ring, because nobody
/// being able to take is the stronger fact), and the copy-in cannot be allowed
/// to fault a process that is not running — so the address was checked at the
/// call by `copy_in_check` and a refusal here stops the machine instead.
///
/// It mints nothing, which is the whole of what a fused call buys: the claim is
/// the parked thread, `PIPE_REPLY_REFS` starts at one for it, and no handle row
/// is spent.
func resume_call_room(t: Int, c: Int) unsafe -> Bool {
    let p = THREADS[t].process
    if PIPES[c].outlet_refs == 0 {
        clear_pending_call(t)
        write_result(frame_slot(t), op_status(SosStatus.PeerClosed))
        THREADS[t].state = ThreadState.Runnable
        ready_push(t)
        return true
    }
    let x = alloc_exchange(c)
    if x == NO_EXCHANGE {
        return false
    }
    let src = THREADS[t].call_src
    let len = THREADS[t].call_len
    let dst = THREADS[t].wait_buf
    if len != 0 {
        if let why = copy_in(p, exchange_body_addr(x), src, len) {
            fatal("parked call body refused by the copy door",
                  (why as UInt8) as UInt, src, p as UInt, ExitCode.ProcessFault)
        }
    }
    PIPE_LENS[x] = len as UInt16
    PIPE_REPLY_REFS[x] = 1
    PIPE_REQUEST_REFS[x] = 0
    PIPE_EX_STATE[x] = ExchangeState.Staged
    stage_push(c, x)
    tie_call_reply(t, x, dst)
    notify_ready(WaitableKind.PipeOutlet, c)
    true
}

/// **THE ORPHANED-CLAIM ARM: a process dying with threads parked in fused
/// calls** (design 10 ruling 4's rider names it; sawos design 17 D-2).
///
/// A parked caller's claim is a reference NO HANDLE TABLE HOLDS, so the
/// teardown's close-all cannot reach it and it would otherwise pin a ring slot
/// — and, worse, leave `PIPE_CALLER` naming a thread slot about to be recycled.
/// This is the arm that drops it, and dropping it IS the abandonment: the claim
/// column falls to zero, which is what `pipe_reply` already reads as
/// abandoned-by-client, so the server's later reply answers `Err(PeerClosed)`
/// with no new vocabulary anywhere.
///
/// **IT RUNS BEFORE THE CLOSE-ALL, AND IN TWO PASSES.** Before, because the
/// close-all drops endpoint columns and every one of those transitions notifies
/// — and a notification must never find a thread of the process that is being
/// torn down. Two passes, because the second one's unrefs CAN deliver: settling
/// an exchange raises the room level, and serving a room waiter of this same
/// dying process would be resuming a thread into a process that no longer has
/// one. So the room waiters go first, which costs nothing at all (a room-parked
/// call holds no exchange and therefore no reference), and by the time any
/// reference is dropped this process has none left for a delivery to find.
public(package) func release_pending_calls(p: Int) unsafe {
    var t: Int = 0
    while t < MAX_THREADS {
        if THREADS[t].process == p && THREADS[t].call_pipe != 0 {
            clear_pending_call(t)
        }
        t = t + 1
    }
    var u: Int = 0
    while u < MAX_THREADS {
        if THREADS[u].process == p && THREADS[u].call_claim != 0 {
            let x = THREADS[u].call_claim - 1
            clear_pending_call(u)
            unref_waitable(WaitableKind.PipeReply, x)
        }
        u = u + 1
    }
}

/// §2.4's signal: accumulate, NEVER block, and tell the Waiter if there is one.
public(package) func signal_event(slot: Int, bits: UInt) unsafe {
    accumulate(slot, bits)
    // A signal of nothing is not a readiness. Both modes can produce it —
    // `signal(0)` in either — and reporting it would wake a waiter for a word
    // it would then read as empty.
    if EVENTS[slot].word == 0 {
        return
    }
    notify_ready(WaitableKind.Event, slot)
}
